coder › Implemented `SlidingWindow` on branch `work/sliding-window`.

What changed:
- `src/window.ts`: per-key timestamp lists backed by a `Map<string, number[]>`. Constructor throws `RangeError` when `limit < 1` or `windowMs < 1`. `allow(key)` prunes events with `t <= now - windowMs` (so an event exactly `windowMs` old no longer counts), returns `false` without recording when at `limit`, else records `now` and returns `true`. `remaining(key)` prunes the same way and returns `limit - fresh.length` (`limit` for unknown keys). Pruned arrays are written back so memory doesn't grow. Matches existing file style (no semicolons, readonly constructor params, default `now = () => Date.now()`).
- `src/window.test.ts` (new): 9 tests using a controlled `clock()` (`now`/`advance` closure), covering allow-up-to-limit, denied-attempt-records-nothing, remaining countdown/recovery, key independence, exact-windowMs expiry boundary (99ms denied / 100ms allowed), sliding partial expiry, both `RangeError` cases, unknown-key remaining.

Checks (exact output):
- `bun test`: `9 pass, 0 fail, 29 expect() calls, Ran 9 tests across 1 file.`
- `tsc --noEmit --strict` on `src/window.ts`: exit 0, no errors. (Full-project `tsc` has no tsconfig in this repo; test file typecheck fails only on `bun:test` module resolution under plain tsc, which is expected — tests run under bun.)
- Lint: no eslint/biome config in repo, nothing to run.

Decisions not in the plan: prune-on-read/write-back to bound memory; `remaining` also prunes for the same reason; `filter(t > cutoff)` implements the "exactly windowMs old no longer counts" rule.

